WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4421.

2
00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.667
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:02.500
<v TERESHA USSIN>His name, right?

4
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:02.778
Sorry.

5
00:00:02.778 --> 00:00:04.167
<v ELIZABETH GELVIN>I don’t have it here.

6
00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:05.000
<v USSIN>Larry Payne?

7
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:05.452
<v LARRY PAYNE>Yes.

8
00:00:05.452 --> 00:00:09.968
<v USSIN>Alright just want to make sure I pronounce everything right.

9
00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:10.419
Okay.

10
00:00:10.419 --> 00:00:12.226
Ready when y’all are.

11
00:00:12.226 --> 00:00:12.677
<v CHARLOTTE WILLCOX>Yeah.

12
00:00:12.677 --> 00:00:13.581
Go ahead.

13
00:00:13.581 --> 00:00:14.032
<v USSIN>Okay.

14
00:00:14.032 --> 00:00:24.419
Today is January twenty-third, I am Teresha Ussin representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

15
00:00:24.419 --> 00:00:33.452
We are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

16
00:00:33.452 --> 00:00:43.839
And to kick that off, we are holding a Mossville oral history day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

17
00:00:43.839 --> 00:00:47.000
I’m here today with Larry Payne.

18
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:47.356
<v PAYNE>Yes.

19
00:00:47.356 --> 00:00:50.200
<v USSIN>Thank you for agreeing to do an interview.

20
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.533
<v PAYNE>Thank you for inviting me, or should I say I’m just glad to be here.

21
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:59.800
<v USSIN>This is going to be a short interview for about fifteen minutes.

22
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:07.978
We will likely be in touch with you later on to set up a much longer interview if you’re interested in continuing on.

23
00:01:07.978 --> 00:01:08.333
<v PAYNE>Okay.

24
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:08.689
<v USSIN>Okay.

25
00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:10.822
Can you state your full name?

26
00:01:10.822 --> 00:01:11.889
<v PAYNE>Larry G. Payne.

27
00:01:11.889 --> 00:01:12.244
<v USSIN>Okay.

28
00:01:12.244 --> 00:01:13.667
Where were you born?

29
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:15.089
<v PAYNE>Right here at Mossville.

30
00:01:15.089 --> 00:01:15.444
<v USSIN>Okay.

31
00:01:15.444 --> 00:01:19.000
What is your parents’ names and your grandparents’ names?

32
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.709
<v PAYNE>My father name is Wilson Payne, Senior.

33
00:01:22.709 --> 00:01:26.948
My mother name is Anna Lee Bernard Payne.

34
00:01:26.948 --> 00:01:27.478
<v USSIN>Okay.

35
00:01:27.478 --> 00:01:30.127
<v PAYNE>My grandfather is George Bernard.

36
00:01:30.127 --> 00:01:32.776
My grandmother is Josephine Bernard.

37
00:01:32.776 --> 00:01:33.306
<v USSIN>Okay.

38
00:01:33.306 --> 00:01:33.836
Alright.

39
00:01:33.836 --> 00:01:38.604
What is your earliest memory of Mossville growing up?

40
00:01:38.604 --> 00:01:39.134
<v PAYNE>Hmm.

41
00:01:39.134 --> 00:01:40.194
Earliest memory.

42
00:01:40.194 --> 00:01:50.791
Well, when I was allowed to come out the house, what I saw was a building here, located right here.

43
00:01:50.791 --> 00:01:59.269
The building, it must have stood about over five foot off the ground with brick pillars.

44
00:01:59.269 --> 00:02:10.925
And there was a fence . . . not a hurricane fence, but an old fence located on the side of the road with steps.

45
00:02:10.925 --> 00:02:27.881
And in order for you to get over the fence you had to cross over some steps that went from one side of the fence to the other side of the fence.

46
00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:30.000
<v USSIN>That is unusual.

47
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.016
<v PAYNE>It is.

48
00:02:31.016 --> 00:02:32.031
It is.

49
00:02:32.031 --> 00:02:32.539
<v USSIN>Okay.

50
00:02:32.539 --> 00:02:44.218
<v PAYNE>And I was told that that building was the original school, but when I came up it was used as a recreation center.

51
00:02:44.218 --> 00:02:47.773
It was used as a recreation center.

52
00:02:47.773 --> 00:02:48.280
<v USSIN>Okay.

53
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.835
<v PAYNE>And I remember us playing in it.

54
00:02:51.835 --> 00:02:54.374
It was a big building.

55
00:02:54.374 --> 00:03:01.991
And I remember playing under the building because it was so high off the ground.

56
00:03:01.991 --> 00:03:11.131
And I remember they had a chimney when they were tearing it down they had bees in it.

57
00:03:11.131 --> 00:03:17.224
And they wanted to . . . They found out that there were a hive.

58
00:03:17.224 --> 00:03:19.763
A bee hive in it.

59
00:03:19.763 --> 00:03:31.950
And they only person that could do . . . That could get the beehive out was an old man live in Mossville they called Pete Moss.

60
00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:37.536
He drove a wagon with horses and his wife named Caledonia.

61
00:03:37.536 --> 00:03:40.583
They had dogs on the wagon.

62
00:03:40.583 --> 00:03:47.692
And this guy, he would come around back in the days we had pigs,

63
00:03:47.692 --> 00:03:54.801
hogs, we would take our waste food and put it outside in a barrel.

64
00:03:54.801 --> 00:04:05.972
He would come around and collect the spoiled waste food and bring it . . . collect it from all the people in the neighborhood.

65
00:04:05.972 --> 00:04:12.573
They knew what they had to do and he would pick it up,

66
00:04:12.573 --> 00:04:20.190
put it on his wagon, and bring it back and feed it to his pigs.

67
00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:23.237
So his name was Pete Moss.

68
00:04:23.237 --> 00:04:34.408
And when they tore the building down, well he was the one that came and got the honey comb from the chimney.

69
00:04:34.408 --> 00:04:36.439
And I remember that.

70
00:04:36.439 --> 00:04:43.040
And when they . . . After they removed . . . They did not tear the building down.

71
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:44.564
They removed it.

72
00:04:44.564 --> 00:04:54.212
They moved it to the site where they built the brand new high school, Mossville High, is still existing.

73
00:04:54.212 --> 00:05:02.844
And they put the building in the back and so this is what I remember about Mossville.

74
00:05:02.844 --> 00:05:05.891
It was not that many traffic.

75
00:05:05.891 --> 00:05:13.000
We had to learn how to cross the road, which it was traffic.

76
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:13.443
<v USSIN>Right.

77
00:05:13.443 --> 00:05:15.213
<v PAYNE>Even as little kids.

78
00:05:15.213 --> 00:05:20.966
Our mother and father, grandmother, grandfather, told us how to cross the road.

79
00:05:20.966 --> 00:05:28.046
They showed us how to cross the road, and then everything else was on our own.

80
00:05:28.046 --> 00:05:30.701
They always say, “Look both ways.

81
00:05:30.701 --> 00:05:41.322
Look both ways,” and my grandmother used to always say, “Y’all don’t play in the road because them car going to pass on y’all.

82
00:05:41.322 --> 00:05:44.420
Them car going to pass on y’all.”

83
00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:45.305
So we

84
00:05:45.305 --> 00:05:46.632
<c.p>always remembered that.

85
00:05:46.632 --> 00:05:51.057
We did not want the cars to pass on us.

86
00:05:51.057 --> 00:05:58.580
So we had to learn how to watch for the cars both ways even as young children.

87
00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:06.103
Because my mother and father was at work and this was the site of the recreation center.

88
00:06:06.103 --> 00:06:06.546
<v USSIN>[Agrees].

89
00:06:06.546 --> 00:06:16.282
<v PAYNE>So we had to come and play . . . We played horseshoe, we played dominos, and other games over here at the recreation center.

90
00:06:16.282 --> 00:06:25.575
And not only that, well that when I was young, but eventually they built . . . This is the second structure right here.

91
00:06:25.575 --> 00:06:27.787
This is the second structure . . .

92
00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:30.000
<v USSIN>For the recreational center?

93
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:30.488
<v PAYNE>Right.

94
00:06:30.488 --> 00:06:30.975
<v USSIN>Okay.

95
00:06:30.975 --> 00:06:41.702
<v PAYNE>They had the original school, they move it, they built the structure, they tore it down, and they built this one here.

96
00:06:41.702 --> 00:06:42.190
<v USSIN>Okay.

97
00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:48.529
<v PAYNE>We had . . . this was always . . . Really the recreation center was always a haven.

98
00:06:48.529 --> 00:06:52.430
A safe haven for the kids in Mossville.

99
00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:58.769
Not only the kids in Mossville, but at one time the kids from

100
00:06:58.769 --> 00:07:05.595
<c.p>Sulphur, from the Sulphur area, they could not interact over there because of segregation.

101
00:07:05.595 --> 00:07:10.959
The kids from Westlake right down the street the same way.

102
00:07:10.959 --> 00:07:19.248
They could not interact, they could not be a part of . . . So they had to come to . . .

103
00:07:19.248 --> 00:07:19.736
<v USSIN>Mossville.

104
00:07:19.736 --> 00:07:29.000
<v PAYNE>Mossville in order for them to receive recreation and interact and that’s how we became . . . everyone knew everybody.

105
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:30.628
<v USSIN>That’s really beautiful.

106
00:07:30.628 --> 00:07:31.171
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

107
00:07:31.171 --> 00:07:42.567
Yeah and then eventually when the school was open, the kids from Westlake, their school only went to the sixth-seventh grade.

108
00:07:42.567 --> 00:07:51.793
So they had to end up coming to Mossville, and that’s how Mossville grew . . . The school grew.

109
00:07:51.793 --> 00:08:01.561
And the kids from Sulphur had to end up . . . Their school only went to the sixth-seventh grade also,

110
00:08:01.561 --> 00:08:05.902
and they had to go . . . come to Mossville.

111
00:08:05.902 --> 00:08:15.671
And that’s how both towns . . . Black towns, black community, knew each other and got to know each other.

112
00:08:15.671 --> 00:08:16.213
<v USSIN>Okay.

113
00:08:16.213 --> 00:08:23.268
Well who were some of the community leaders that you remember growing up?

114
00:08:23.268 --> 00:08:29.238
<v PAYNE>The community leaders has always been, I was told, Mr. Rigmaiden.

115
00:08:29.238 --> 00:08:37.378
I’m not sure if . . . There were many and I’m not exactly sure of the name.

116
00:08:37.378 --> 00:08:43.890
Jake Rigmaiden . . . I’m not sure about the individuals, but it was always,

117
00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:50.402
excuse me, the Rigmaiden who were spokesmen or leader in the community.

118
00:08:50.402 --> 00:08:50.945
<v USSIN>Okay.

119
00:08:50.945 --> 00:08:54.744
Now you spoke a lot of livestock.

120
00:08:54.744 --> 00:08:58.000
Did you have a garden?

121
00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.059
<v PAYNE>Oh, yes.

122
00:08:59.059 --> 00:09:01.706
We always had a garden.

123
00:09:01.706 --> 00:09:04.882
My grandfather, he would raise chicken.

124
00:09:04.882 --> 00:09:10.706
A matter of fact, my grandfather, I’m sorry for going back.

125
00:09:10.706 --> 00:09:11.765
<v USSIN>It’s okay.

126
00:09:11.765 --> 00:09:20.235
<v PAYNE>He came from Carencro, Louisiana and I’m going to assume that’s the reason why they came . . .

127
00:09:20.235 --> 00:09:21.824
<v USSIN>Here to Mossville.

128
00:09:21.824 --> 00:09:22.353
<v PAYNE>Here.

129
00:09:22.353 --> 00:09:27.647
My . . . I think my uncle, my daddy brother move here,

130
00:09:27.647 --> 00:09:35.588
and then I’m assume . . . I wasn’t living, I assume that my brother followed his brother.

131
00:09:35.588 --> 00:09:46.176
And when my father came and my mother, I’m assume once again that my . . . Because she was the only child.

132
00:09:46.176 --> 00:09:54.118
So my grandfather and grandmother moved from Carencro, Louisiana to be with their . . . her daughter.

133
00:09:54.118 --> 00:09:59.941
Because at the age of thirty-eight my mom had thirteen babies.

134
00:09:59.941 --> 00:10:01.000
<v USSIN>Wow.

135
00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.077
<v PAYNE>So she was having babies almost every year.

136
00:10:04.077 --> 00:10:04.462
<v USSIN>Yeah.

137
00:10:04.462 --> 00:10:14.077
<v PAYNE>And my . . . I’m a just assume that my grandmother knew that . . . she knew that she couldn’t . . . She was going to need help with those babies.

138
00:10:14.077 --> 00:10:14.462
<v USSIN>Yeah.

139
00:10:14.462 --> 00:10:14.846
Definitely.

140
00:10:14.846 --> 00:10:15.231
Yeah.

141
00:10:15.231 --> 00:10:18.308
<v PAYNE>So but my grandfather was a railroad man.

142
00:10:18.308 --> 00:10:20.231
He retired from Southern Pacific.

143
00:10:20.231 --> 00:10:26.000
And every week he would go to work, he’d catch a train in Lake Charles,

144
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:31.385
he’ll go to work and get back on the train where he rode free.

145
00:10:31.385 --> 00:10:39.846
Him, and my mother, and my sibling that was old enough, they’d get free pass because he was employee at Southern Pacific.

146
00:10:39.846 --> 00:10:40.231
<v USSIN>Employee.

147
00:10:40.231 --> 00:10:41.000
Right.

148
00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:47.517
<v PAYNE>And on the weekend he would get back on the train and go back to work at Carencro,

149
00:10:47.517 --> 00:10:52.948
and on the weekend he’d come back and he done that for a long time.

150
00:10:52.948 --> 00:10:56.931
He was a dedicated man to even do such a thing.

151
00:10:56.931 --> 00:11:00.914
And back at that time, they did not use modern technology.

152
00:11:00.914 --> 00:11:02.000
<v USSIN>Of course.

153
00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.941
<v PAYNE>To lay those tracks down.

154
00:11:03.941 --> 00:11:04.329
<v USSIN>Right.

155
00:11:04.329 --> 00:11:05.494
<v PAYNE>It was hard.

156
00:11:05.494 --> 00:11:07.435
He was a big man.

157
00:11:07.435 --> 00:11:11.706
Stand about six two, six three, and very knowledgeable about everything.

158
00:11:11.706 --> 00:11:14.812
And I’ve always wanted to be like him.

159
00:11:14.812 --> 00:11:19.471
But going back to the garden, yes, he always had a garden.

160
00:11:19.471 --> 00:11:20.635
He raised chicken.

161
00:11:20.635 --> 00:11:23.741
He would get his chicken from George Therioux.

162
00:11:23.741 --> 00:11:24.906
I remember that.

163
00:11:24.906 --> 00:11:25.682
Little biddies.

164
00:11:25.682 --> 00:11:29.565
He would go get them biddies and raise them chicken.

165
00:11:29.565 --> 00:11:33.059
And it was my grandmother job to kill them.

166
00:11:33.059 --> 00:11:33.447
<v USSIN>[Laughs].

167
00:11:33.447 --> 00:11:35.000
What a job.

168
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.493
<v PAYNE>And she would ring the chicken neck.

169
00:11:37.493 --> 00:11:43.192
And she would get in and ring the chicken neck and she would get in there,

170
00:11:43.192 --> 00:11:46.397
bake that chicken, fry that chicken, and it was . . .

171
00:11:46.397 --> 00:11:47.822
<v USSIN>That’s how it’s done.

172
00:11:47.822 --> 00:11:48.178
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

173
00:11:48.178 --> 00:11:49.959
It was a lovely lovely . . .

174
00:11:49.959 --> 00:11:53.164
<v USSIN>I’m assuming that you went to elementary school here.

175
00:11:53.164 --> 00:11:53.521
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

176
00:11:53.521 --> 00:11:55.301
In fact, yes I did.

177
00:11:55.301 --> 00:11:56.014
In Mossville.

178
00:11:56.014 --> 00:11:57.082
<v USSIN>Graduated high school . . .

179
00:11:57.082 --> 00:11:57.795
<v PAYNE>High school.

180
00:11:57.795 --> 00:12:00.288
I ended up graduating from Westlake High.

181
00:12:00.288 --> 00:12:01.000
<v USSIN>Okay.

182
00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:05.935
<v PAYNE>The last year Mossville . . . well, I’m not going to say existed.

183
00:12:05.935 --> 00:12:10.869
They closed . . . Well, we were sent to Westlake High in ’69-’70.

184
00:12:10.869 --> 00:12:11.318
<v USSIN>Okay.

185
00:12:11.318 --> 00:12:16.252
<v PAYNE>But later on they reopened it up for a junior high.

186
00:12:16.252 --> 00:12:18.495
It was a junior high.

187
00:12:18.495 --> 00:12:18.944
<v USSIN>Okay.

188
00:12:18.944 --> 00:12:21.636
<v PAYNE>But’69-’70 I was in Westlake High.

189
00:12:21.636 --> 00:12:22.084
<v USSIN>Okay.

190
00:12:22.084 --> 00:12:27.019
Do you remember . . . going back to those youthful high school days,

191
00:12:27.019 --> 00:12:32.402
do you remember what the young . . . Where the young folks would gather?

192
00:12:32.402 --> 00:12:35.093
<v PAYNE>Where did the young people gather?

193
00:12:35.093 --> 00:12:35.542
<v USSIN>Gather.

194
00:12:35.542 --> 00:12:35.991
Yes.

195
00:12:35.991 --> 00:12:37.785
<v PAYNE>We had . . . Oh, look.

196
00:12:37.785 --> 00:12:39.131
We had everything.

197
00:12:39.131 --> 00:12:42.720
We had no need to go out anywhere.

198
00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:44.514
We had a canteen.

199
00:12:44.514 --> 00:12:49.000
I don’t know if you ever heard of canteen.

200
00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.338
<v USSIN>No I haven’t.

201
00:12:50.338 --> 00:12:53.905
Could you kind of explain a little bit?

202
00:12:53.905 --> 00:12:58.811
<v PAYNE>We had canteen where private people would have a little business.

203
00:12:58.811 --> 00:12:59.257
<v USSIN>Okay.

204
00:12:59.257 --> 00:13:05.946
<v PAYNE>And it design just for the youth to come on a Saturday evening or Sunday.

205
00:13:05.946 --> 00:13:07.730
They’ll have a jukebox.

206
00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:08.176
<v USSIN>Okay.

207
00:13:08.176 --> 00:13:15.311
<v PAYNE>And we would gather there and we would play music, and dance, and things like that.

208
00:13:15.311 --> 00:13:18.432
There were one in . . . Down the street.

209
00:13:18.432 --> 00:13:21.108
It was called the Pirate’s Den.

210
00:13:21.108 --> 00:13:22.000
<v USSIN>Alright.

211
00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:27.256
<v PAYNE>The Pirates was named . . . It was the name of our mascot.

212
00:13:27.256 --> 00:13:28.211
Mossville Pirate.

213
00:13:28.211 --> 00:13:28.689
<v USSIN>Okay.

214
00:13:28.689 --> 00:13:32.033
<v PAYNE>So they call it the Pirate’s Den.

215
00:13:32.033 --> 00:13:32.511
<v USSIN>Den.

216
00:13:32.511 --> 00:13:32.989
Okay.

217
00:13:32.989 --> 00:13:35.378
<v PAYNE>And they had another canteen.

218
00:13:35.378 --> 00:13:38.722
I don’t know the name of it.

219
00:13:38.722 --> 00:13:42.544
It was ran by Ms. Risa Mae Mouton.

220
00:13:42.544 --> 00:13:47.322
And then they had another canteen ran by Mr. Jennings.

221
00:13:47.322 --> 00:13:55.922
So, and then they had a Dairy Queen before Dairy Queen even . . . The name Dairy Queen showed up.

222
00:13:55.922 --> 00:13:58.789
We had the first Dairy Queen.

223
00:13:58.789 --> 00:14:00.222
<v USSIN>A Dairy Queen.

224
00:14:00.222 --> 00:14:00.700
<v PAYNE>Here.

225
00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:02.611
<v USSIN>Dairy Queen in Mossville.

226
00:14:02.611 --> 00:14:03.089
<v PAYNE>Right.

227
00:14:03.089 --> 00:14:04.044
In Mossville.

228
00:14:04.044 --> 00:14:05.000
<v USSIN>Wow.

229
00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:11.056
<v PAYNE>Not only that, right . . . That vacant lot, our home is right here.

230
00:14:11.056 --> 00:14:12.570
There’s another home.

231
